<section class="wr-hero"><div class="wr-tri"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794471885837-3f807636f8c6?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=640&q=75" alt="woman in a black long-sleeved shirt and light blue trousers" width="480" height="640" fetchpriority="high"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794240481054-77821beec18b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=640&q=75" alt="person in a blue and yellow pleated dress" width="480" height="640" fetchpriority="high"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794345527565-77afad1efb2a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=640&q=75" alt="man in a striped t-shirt and beige shorts walking" width="480" height="640" fetchpriority="high"></div><div class="wr-w"><div class="wr-hero-c"><p class="wr-k">Issue 01 &mdash; Getting dressed, simplified</p><h1>Fewer decisions.<br><i>Better outfits.</i></h1><p class="wr-lead">Wearora is an independent style guide for real wardrobes. Learn the outfit formulas that work, the proportions that flatter and the pieces your wardrobe is missing.</p><p class="wr-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Build an outfit</a><a class="wr-u" href="outfit-formulas-guide.html">Read the formulas</a></p></div></div></section>

<section class="wr-s"><div class="wr-w"><div class="wr-sh"><span>A</span><h2>Four formulas worth memorising</h2></div><div class="wr-forms">
<article><p class="wr-sw"><i style="background:#1E2A44"></i><i style="background:#F3EFE7"></i><i style="background:#7A5434"></i></p><h3>The easy weekday</h3><p>Straight jeans + fine knit + loafers or clean trainers.</p></article>
<article><p class="wr-sw"><i style="background:#111"></i><i style="background:#fff"></i><i style="background:#C9B79C"></i></p><h3>The sharp meeting</h3><p>Tailored trousers + crisp shirt + unstructured blazer.</p></article>
<article><p class="wr-sw"><i style="background:#2643E9"></i><i style="background:#E9E3D6"></i><i style="background:#3B3B3B"></i></p><h3>The weekend layer</h3><p>T-shirt + overshirt or cardigan + wide trousers.</p></article>
<article><p class="wr-sw"><i style="background:#5B1F2E"></i><i style="background:#D8C6B5"></i><i style="background:#111"></i></p><h3>The evening shift</h3><p>Slip dress or silk top + long coat + one bold accessory.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="wr-tl" id="tools"><div class="wr-w"><div class="wr-sh wr-sh-l"><span>B</span><h2>Style tools</h2></div><div class="wr-tools">
<div class="wr-tool"><h3><small>01</small>Outfit Formula Builder</h3><label for="of-o">Where are you going?<select id="of-o"><option value="work">Office</option><option value="casual" selected>Casual day out</option><option value="dinner">Dinner</option><option value="travel">Travel day</option><option value="event">Daytime event</option></select></label><label for="of-w">Weather<select id="of-w"><option value="hot">Hot</option><option value="mild" selected>Mild</option><option value="cold">Cold</option></select></label><label for="of-v">Your vibe<select id="of-v"><option value="classic">Classic</option><option value="relaxed">Relaxed</option><option value="bold">Bold</option></select></label><div class="wr-out" id="of-out" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="wr-tool"><h3><small>02</small>Proportion Balancer</h3><label for="pb-t">Top<select id="pb-t"><option value="fit">Fitted</option><option value="reg" selected>Regular</option><option value="over">Oversized</option><option value="crop">Cropped</option></select></label><label for="pb-b">Bottom<select id="pb-b"><option value="slim">Slim / skinny</option><option value="straight" selected>Straight</option><option value="wide">Wide-leg</option><option value="skirt">Midi skirt</option><option value="short">Shorts</option></select></label><div class="wr-out" id="pb-out" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="wr-tool"><h3><small>03</small>Wardrobe Gap Finder</h3><p class="wr-tp">Tick what you already own.</p><div class="wr-ck" id="wg-c"><label><input type="checkbox" value="jeans" checked> Dark jeans</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="trousers"> Tailored trousers</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="tee" checked> Plain T-shirts</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="shirt"> White shirt</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="knit" checked> Fine knit</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="blazer"> Blazer</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="coat"> Long coat</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="shoes"> Smart shoes</label></div><div class="wr-out" id="wg-out" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="wr-s"><div class="wr-w wr-prop"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794259677539-1c44fc7e30c8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=700&q=75" alt="woman in a black t-shirt and jeans on stone steps" width="560" height="700" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A short top over long trousers: the one-third, two-thirds split.</figcaption></figure><div><div class="wr-sh wr-sh-l"><span>C</span><h2>The rule of thirds</h2></div><p class="wr-lead">Outfits look balanced when they divide the body unevenly. One-third to two-thirds almost always looks better than a half-and-half split.</p><div class="wr-thirds"><div><b>&#8531; / &#8532;</b><span>Short or tucked top, long trousers or skirt</span></div><div><b>&#8532; / &#8531;</b><span>Long top or dress, short visible leg or skirt</span></div><div><b>&frac12; / &frac12;</b><span>Hip-length top cutting the body in half: try a tuck</span></div></div></div></div></section>

<section class="wr-band"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794301683133-6f764a79f1de?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&h=560&q=75" alt="mannequins wearing wool coats and a plaid beret" width="1400" height="560" loading="lazy"><div class="wr-band-t"><p class="wr-k">Our view</p><p class="wr-q">&ldquo;Style is mostly repetition. Find what works, then wear it well, again and again.&rdquo;</p></div></section>

<section class="wr-s"><div class="wr-w"><div class="wr-sh"><span>D</span><h2>The guides</h2></div><div class="wr-gds"><a href="outfit-formulas-guide.html"><div class="wr-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794660566799-7aabbe08e20b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=460&q=75" alt="woman in a black cape and skirt sitting on a stone wall" width="640" height="460" loading="lazy"></div><p class="wr-k">Guide 01</p><h3>The Outfit Formulas Guide</h3><p>Twelve tested formulas for work, weekends and evenings, and how to make them your own.</p></a><a href="proportion-guide.html"><div class="wr-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794579739866-8be897d14fd7?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=460&q=75" alt="woman in a black turtleneck leaning against a wall" width="640" height="460" loading="lazy"></div><p class="wr-k">Guide 02</p><h3>The Proportion Guide</h3><p>Lengths, volumes and tucks: how to make any outfit look balanced.</p></a></div></div></section>

<section class="wr-s wr-gray"><div class="wr-w wr-fq"><div><div class="wr-sh wr-sh-l"><span>E</span><h2>Asked &amp; answered</h2></div><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794776296961-3ccc688cf0d5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=460&h=560&q=75" alt="woman in a beige puffer jacket and scarves" width="460" height="560" loading="lazy"></div><div class="wr-faq"><details open><summary>What is an outfit formula?</summary><p>A repeatable recipe for getting dressed, such as &ldquo;straight jeans + fitted knit + loafers&rdquo;. Once you know a few formulas that suit you, you can swap colours and pieces in and out and still look put together.</p></details><details><summary>How do I balance a loose top?</summary><p>Pair volume with something slimmer. A relaxed shirt or oversized jumper usually looks best with straight or slim trousers, a pencil skirt or a front tuck that shows the waist.</p></details><details><summary>How many pieces do I really need?</summary><p>Far fewer than most people own. A working wardrobe can be built from around 25&ndash;35 pieces per season if most of them work together in more than one outfit.</p></details><details><summary>What does &ldquo;proportion&rdquo; mean in style?</summary><p>How the lengths and volumes of your clothes divide your body. A common, flattering split is roughly one-third to two-thirds, for example a short top over long trousers.</p></details><details><summary>How can I make basics look less boring?</summary><p>Change one element: texture (knit, leather, linen), a strong colour, an interesting shoe, or a tuck or roll that changes the shape. Small changes read as intentional.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need to follow trends?</summary><p>No. Trends are useful for ideas, but a wardrobe built around shapes and colours that suit you will last longer and need fewer replacements.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="wr-nl"><div class="wr-w wr-nl-g"><h2>The Wearora Fold</h2><p>One outfit formula and one wardrobe fix, once a month.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@wearora.com?subject=The%20Wearora%20Fold&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

